A||||3||MooCow||Replacing door locks?||||11||
Z||000000||MooCow||01-15-2001||06:19 PM||||I heard that it is a big pain in the A$$ to fix a door lock?  On the drivers side the key will go in but won't unlock or lock it up. All it does is twist.  How much for a new one and how hard is it to replace?  Also any special tools needed to complete the job?<P>                             Thanks Ken||||reg||11
Z||000001||sqoach||01-15-2001||07:15 PM||||yes, pain in the a$$. i think i bought a set for about $20. i can't remember. only thing i remember is that it took quite a while. i don't remember any "special" tools that i used. i only replaced the driver's side lock because it was the only one messed up. i'll probably do the passenger's side when it warms up and i have enough time. good luck.<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-15-2001||07:50 PM||||probably the linkage fell of the back. check that first.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||01-16-2001||08:44 AM||||I agree with Theogre.  If the key turns but nothing happens the link rod probably became detached from the lock arm.  It's not hard to fix if you have patience.<P>I picked up an assortment of link rod end clips for GM cars at a local parts store for a few bucks.  Long needle nose pliers helps.<P>You'll have to remove the inner door panel and raise the window to get access to the back side of the lock.  Be careful when popping the door panel off because there is a puch in fastener around the middle of the door near the arm rest.  If you break it off of the door panel you can hot glue it back on.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
